# =============================================================================
# CODEOWNERS — who reviews what, and which files cannot be changed casually
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hot path. A change here decides what the bot pays.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `curve.rs` and `pricing.rs` decide the price. `risk.rs` decides whether a
# trade happens at all. `pumpfun.rs` decides whether a launch is real. Between
# them they are the code that moves money, and each is the kind of file where a
# plausible-looking change is silently catastrophic: an inverted comparison in
# the constant-product maths still returns a number, and the number is simply
# wrong.
#
# These paths are deliberately not automated away. `cargo clippy` catches
# style; it cannot catch an inverted comparison that still type-checks.
/crates/core/src/pricing.rs   @sanot-tech
/crates/core/src/curve.rs     @sanot-tech
/crates/core/src/risk.rs      @sanot-tech
/crates/core/src/venue/       @sanot-tech
/crates/core/src/stream/      @sanot-tech

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The configuration surface. A change here changes what the bot is allowed to
# do, so it deserves the same scrutiny as the maths it feeds.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/crates/core/src/config.rs    @sanot-tech

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The promotion gate itself.
#
# `crates/sandbox` is listed not because it is delicate -- it is a test crate
# with no production path -- but because it is the only place where instructions
# are actually executed. A change that makes the sandbox accept a malformed
# program, or makes its output depend on iteration order, would not fail any
# test that does not run the VM. It would make the golden snapshots in chapter 4
# meaningless, quietly.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/crates/sandbox/              @sanot-tech

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test files that assert a *claim* rather than an implementation detail.
#
# Named individually so that weakening one is a visible act. These files are the
# record of what has been proved; deleting an assertion to make a build green
# should require explaining what was being asserted.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/crates/core/tests/math_invariants.rs    @sanot-tech
/crates/core/tests/decode_matrix.rs      @sanot-tech
/crates/core/tests/risk_sandbox.rs       @sanot-tech
/crates/telemetry/tests/                  @sanot-tech

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The gates.
#
# A workflow that fails to parse looks exactly like one that failed, and the
# difference is only visible in the API. Two of these were wrong in ways that
# showed up as a red X rather than as an error message; `uses:` without a
# space after the colon was one of them. Changes here are reviewed by the same
# person precisely because a silent gate is worse than a loud one.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.github/                    @sanot-tech
/deny.toml                   @sanot-tech
/tools/                      @sanot-tech
/.cargo/config.toml          @sanot-tech

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: everything else.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*   @sanot-tech